@node gpui-pretext Manual
@chapter Crate @code{gpui-pretext}
@cindex gpui-pretext
@cindex text layout

@code{gpui-pretext} is a high-performance text measurement and
multiline layout library, a Rust port of @code{chenglou/pretext}. It
is what makes resizable panels in the SotF desktop player feel
instant: line-breaking is pure arithmetic on cached widths, not a
re-measurement.

@menu
* gpui-pretext User Guide::
* gpui-pretext Developer Guide::
@end menu

@node gpui-pretext User Guide
@section User Guide

End users see the result -- smooth, jank-free re-wrap when resizing a
panel containing labels, track titles or long descriptions. There is
no user-facing configuration.

@node gpui-pretext Developer Guide
@section Developer Guide

@subheading Two-phase model

@enumerate
@item @strong{Prepare} (@code{prepare} / @code{prepare_with_segments})
  segments the text and measures segment widths via the
  @code{TextMeasure} trait. Run once per text block.
@item @strong{Layout} (@code{layout} / @code{layout_with_lines} /
  @code{layout_next_line}) is pure arithmetic over cached widths.
  Fast enough to run on every resize.
@end enumerate

@subheading TextMeasure trait

Backend-agnostic: implement @code{measure_width(&str) -> f64} against
whatever rendering layer you have. The SotF app provides an impl that
uses CoreText / DirectWrite / FreeType through GPUI.

@subheading Example

@example
struct MyMeasure;
impl TextMeasure for MyMeasure @{
    fn measure_width(&self, text: &str) -> f64 @{
        // delegate to a real text renderer
        text.len() as f64 * 8.0
    @}
@}

let prepared = prepare(text, &MyMeasure, PrepareOptions::default());
let layout   = layout(&prepared, available_width);
@end example

@subheading Use in @code{gpui-builder}

The constraint layout solver calls into @code{TextMeasure} so that
slots can be sized from their text content
(@ref{gpui-builder Manual}).

@subheading Testing

@example
cargo test -p gpui-pretext
@end example
